    "def": "Compression is done by category, not by taste. Illustrations, repetitions and rhetorical decoration go first; the argument's claims and its connectives stay. Cutting in a fixed order stops you removing something essential under time pressure.",
    "exp": "Cut in this order and the one-third target usually arrives on its own.\n\nFirst, examples and illustrations. A passage that says manners are learnt at home and then spends four sentences on a child copying a parent has given you one idea and one picture of it. Keep the idea, drop the picture. Where several examples make the same point, replace them with the generalisation they share.\n\nSecond, repetition. Formal prose restates its thesis at the start and again at the end, often in different words. Say it once. Watch for the same idea returning inside a paragraph in a slightly altered form.\n\nThird, rhetoric. Rhetorical questions become statements. Quotations, proverbs, direct address to the reader and figures of speech all go. So do intensifiers - 'very', 'extremely', 'truly' - which carry attitude but no content.\n\nWhat never gets cut: the controlling idea, each distinct supporting claim, any qualification the author made, and the connectives that hold them together. If cutting has left a list of statements with no 'although' or 'because', you have compressed away the argument itself and kept only its conclusions.",
