    "def": "A precis is written in the third person and in reported speech, in the past tense where the original reports or argues. First-person pronouns from the passage become third-person references to the author, and direct speech becomes indirect.",
    "exp": "If the passage says 'I have always believed that our schools neglect this', the precis does not repeat 'I' or 'our'. It says the writer believed that schools neglected it. This is not a stylistic preference - it follows from the precis being your report of someone else's argument rather than a reproduction of it.\n\nThe conversions are mechanical once you see them. First person becomes third: I becomes the writer or the author, we and our become they and their or a named group. Direct speech loses its quotation marks and takes 'that'. Present tense in reported argument generally shifts to past. Words tied to the speaker's position - here, now, today, this - become there, then, at that time, that.\n\nOne caution. Where the passage states a permanent truth or a general fact rather than a personal claim, the present tense is normally kept: a passage explaining that water boils at a hundred degrees does not become 'water boiled'. Shift the tense of the reporting, not of standing facts.\n\nAnd never let your own voice in. No 'in my view', no 'we can see that'. The only person present in a precis is the author of the passage, referred to in the third person.",
