    "sub": "A worked precis, start to finish",
    "def": "A complete example: a source passage, the precis made from it at roughly one third, and a note on what was cut and why. Reading one precis being made teaches more than any list of rules.",
    "exp": "THE PASSAGE (about 210 words)\n\nEvery town in the hills owes more to its buses than its residents usually admit. Before the first regular service ran, a journey to the district headquarters meant a full day on foot, and for many families it meant simply not going at all. Traders carried what they could lift. Patients arrived late or not at all. When the buses came, the change was not merely one of speed; it altered who was able to participate in public life. A farmer could sell in a market thirty kilometres away and return the same evening. A student could attend a school her village could not support.\n\nYet public transport is persistently undervalued, because its benefits are diffuse and its failures are visible. A late bus is noticed by everyone waiting for it; the hundred journeys that ran to time are noticed by nobody. Planners, who see only the subsidy in the accounts, are tempted to treat the service as a cost to be trimmed. This is a mistake. The correct comparison is not between the subsidy and zero, but between the subsidy and the cost of the isolation that would return without it.\n\nTHE PRECIS (70 words - one third)\n\nTitle: The Undervalued Hill Bus\n\nThe writer argued that regular bus services transformed hill towns, replacing day-long journeys on foot with same-day travel and so widening access to markets, schools and medical care. He held that the service was nonetheless undervalued, because its successes went unnoticed while its failures did not, and planners saw only the subsidy. The proper comparison, he maintained, was with the cost of the isolation that would otherwise return.\n\nWHAT WAS CUT AND WHY\n\nThe illustrations went first: the trader lifting goods, the farmer thirty kilometres away, the student and her village. Each pictures a single idea - that the buses widened participation - which the precis states once. The late bus and the hundred punctual journeys are one illustration of one point, so they became 'its successes went unnoticed while its failures did not'.\n\nThe argument's shape was kept. 'Yet' survives as 'nonetheless', and the closing correction survives as 'the proper comparison'. Without those two joints the precis would be three assertions instead of an argument.\n\nNote the person and tense: 'the writer argued', 'he held', 'he maintained' - third person, reported, past.",
