    "id": "c42",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "Emerging Technologies: Artificial Intelligence (AI), Internet of Things (IoT), Blockchain, Big Data, Cloud Computing and Edge Computing",
    "def": "Six technologies the syllabus groups together. AI makes machines perform tasks that would need human reasoning. IoT puts networked sensors into ordinary physical objects. Blockchain is a distributed, append-only ledger whose blocks are cryptographically chained. Big data is data too large, too fast or too varied for conventional tools. Cloud computing rents computing as an on-demand service. Edge computing pushes that processing back close to where the data is produced.",
    "exp": "AI covers systems that learn from data or apply rules to classify, predict or generate. Machine learning is a subset of AI, and deep learning using neural networks is a subset of machine learning. Generative AI and large language models are the current mainstream form.\n\nIoT devices carry sensors, embedded processing and connectivity, reporting readings and accepting remote commands: smart electricity meters, streetlights, soil sensors, wearables. IoT is a major source of big data.\n\nBlockchain groups transactions into blocks, each storing the hash of the previous block, replicated across nodes and agreed by a consensus mechanism. Altering one block breaks every hash after it, which is what makes it tamper-evident. Public chains such as Bitcoin are permissionless; government land-registry and certificate pilots use permissioned chains.\n\nBig data is characterised by the Vs: Volume, Velocity and Variety, with Veracity and Value commonly added. It is handled by distributed frameworks such as Hadoop, HDFS, MapReduce and Spark.\n\nCloud versus edge is the distinction worth learning properly. Cloud centralises computation in large remote data centres, sold pay-as-you-go and scaled elastically; you accept network latency and bandwidth cost in exchange for capacity. Edge does the reverse, processing on or beside the device so a response takes milliseconds and raw data need not leave the site. They are complementary: the edge filters and reacts, the cloud trains models and stores history. Fog computing is the intermediate layer.",
    "facts": [
      "Machine learning is a subset of AI, and deep learning is a subset of machine learning.",
      "Big data's three core Vs are Volume, Velocity and Variety; Veracity and Value are commonly added.",
      "Each blockchain block stores the cryptographic hash of the previous block, which is what makes tampering detectable.",
      "Cloud service models are IaaS, PaaS and SaaS; deployment models are public, private, hybrid and community.",
      "Edge computing's primary benefits are reduced latency and reduced bandwidth use, achieved by processing data near its source.",
      "Fog computing sits between the edge devices and the cloud as an intermediate processing layer."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Cloud versus edge: both are off-the-desktop computing, but cloud is centralised and distant while edge is distributed and local. Latency-critical work such as a factory safety cut-off or an autonomous vehicle belongs at the edge.",
      "AI, machine learning and deep learning are nested, not parallel alternatives. Any question listing them side by side is testing the nesting.",
      "Blockchain is not cryptocurrency. Cryptocurrency is one application of blockchain; land records, supply chain and certificates are others.",
      "Big data is defined by the Vs, not by a threshold in gigabytes. There is no size number that makes data \"big\", and IoT feeds it rather than being a kind of it."
    ],
    "mnem": "Big data's three Vs: Volume, Velocity, Variety.",
    "rel": [
      "Digital Transformation and e-Governance",
      "Green Computing and Sustainable IT",
      "Classification of Computers"
    ]
