    "id": "c43",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "Green Computing and Sustainable IT",
    "def": "Green computing is the design, manufacture, use and disposal of computers and related systems with minimum environmental impact: cutting energy consumption, reducing hazardous materials, and handling end-of-life equipment responsibly. Sustainable IT extends this across the whole lifecycle and supply chain, and also covers using IT to reduce emissions in other sectors.",
    "exp": "Four pillars are conventionally listed: green design, green manufacturing, green use and green disposal.\n\nIn practice the measures are virtualisation and server consolidation, so that one physical host runs many virtual machines instead of a rack of under-used servers; migration to cloud data centres that run at higher utilisation; power management such as sleep, hibernate, CPU throttling and display timeout; thin clients in place of full desktops; efficient cooling with hot and cold aisle containment; buying certified equipment; and reducing printing.\n\nThe standard data-centre metric is PUE, Power Usage Effectiveness, calculated as total facility power divided by IT equipment power. The theoretical ideal is 1.0 and lower is better.\n\nCertifications and standards worth naming: ENERGY STAR from the US EPA and EPEAT both rate energy efficiency and environmental performance, while RoHS restricts hazardous substances such as lead, mercury, cadmium and hexavalent chromium in electrical and electronic equipment.\n\nFor e-waste in India, the governing instrument is the E-Waste (Management) Rules made under the Environment (Protection) Act. They are built on Extended Producer Responsibility, which makes the producer accountable for collecting and recycling end-of-life products through collection centres and authorised dismantlers and recyclers. The Central Pollution Control Board is the implementing regulator.\n\nFinally, distinguish greening of IT, making the IT itself less wasteful, from greening by IT, using video-conferencing, e-governance and smart grids to cut emissions elsewhere.",
    "facts": [
      "PUE = total facility energy divided by IT equipment energy; 1.0 is the theoretical ideal and lower values are better.",
      "The four pillars of green computing are green design, green manufacturing, green use and green disposal.",
      "RoHS restricts hazardous substances in electronic equipment; ENERGY STAR and EPEAT rate energy efficiency and environmental performance.",
      "India's E-Waste (Management) Rules operate on Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR), with the Central Pollution Control Board as regulator.",
      "Virtualisation and server consolidation give the largest practical energy saving in a server room, by raising utilisation of fewer physical machines."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "PUE is inverted by candidates constantly. Lower is better: a PUE of 2.0 means as much power goes to cooling and losses as to the computers themselves.",
      "Green computing is not only about saving electricity. Disposal and hazardous materials are equally part of the definition.",
      "ENERGY STAR rates energy efficiency, RoHS restricts hazardous substances. Different purposes, routinely swapped in options.",
      "Greening of IT versus greening by IT: replacing a CRT with an LED monitor is greening of IT; replacing travel with video-conferencing is greening by IT."
    ],
    "mnem": "Four greens: Design, Manufacture, Use, Dispose.",
    "rel": [
      "Emerging Technologies: Artificial Intelligence (AI), Internet of Things (IoT), Blockchain, Big Data, Cloud Computing and Edge Computing",
      "Digital Transformation and e-Governance"
    ]
