    "id": "c57",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "Storage Area Network (SAN)",
    "def": "A SAN is a separate, dedicated high-speed network whose only job is to carry storage traffic between servers and shared storage arrays. It moves data as blocks, carrying SCSI or NVMe commands over Fibre Channel or over TCP/IP as iSCSI. A slice of array capacity, called a LUN, is presented to a server and appears to that server's operating system as a local, unformatted disk.",
    "exp": "Everything follows from block-level access. The array hands over raw blocks; the server itself partitions the LUN, puts a file system on it and mounts it. That is why a SAN suits databases, virtual-machine datastores, mail stores and any workload that wants disk-like latency and its own file system, and why the operating system cannot tell a SAN LUN from an internal drive.\n\nA server joins a Fibre Channel SAN through a host bus adapter (HBA) and FC switches; an iSCSI SAN needs only a standard NIC and an iSCSI initiator, which is why iSCSI is the affordable route for small and mid-size sites. Zoning on the switches and LUN masking on the array decide which server may see which LUN - the access control layer that replaces file permissions.\n\nBecause the array is shared, a SAN enables features that DAS cannot: boot from SAN, live migration of virtual machines between hosts, clustering, array-level snapshots and replication to a DR site.\n\nThe price is cost and skill. Fibre Channel means separate switches, adapters and cabling and staff who understand zoning. Newer deployments increasingly use NVMe over Fabrics for lower latency.",
    "facts": [
      "SAN is block-level storage; the server, not the array, creates the file system",
      "A LUN (logical unit number) is the unit of capacity presented to a host",
      "Fibre Channel hosts connect via an HBA; iSCSI hosts need only a standard NIC and an iSCSI initiator",
      "Zoning (on the fabric) and LUN masking (on the array) control which host sees which LUN",
      "SAN enables boot from SAN, server clustering, VM live migration and array-based replication"
    ],
    "traps": [
      "SAN vs NAS - SAN gives BLOCKS over a dedicated fabric and looks like a local disk; NAS gives FILES over the ordinary LAN and looks like a shared folder",
      "iSCSI runs over ordinary Ethernet but is still a SAN - the access level (block vs file), not the cable, decides which it is",
      "Two servers writing to the same LUN will corrupt it unless a cluster-aware file system is used; sharing a file is a NAS job",
      "SAN expands to Storage Area Network, not Storage Attached Network"
    ],
    "mnem": "",
    "rel": [
      "Network Attached Storage (NAS)",
      "Disaster Recovery Concepts",
      "HDD, SSD and NVMe Storage"
    ]
