    "id": "c78",
    "paper": "TECH1",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Fundamentals of Computer",
    "sub": "Electronic Records management",
    "def": "Electronic records management is the systematic control of records held in digital form across their whole life - capture, classification, storage, retrieval, retention and final disposal or permanent preservation - so that they remain authentic, reliable, complete and usable for as long as they are needed. A record is evidence of a transaction or decision: in government it is the file, the noting, the order, the sanction and the correspondence that prove what was done and by whom.",
    "exp": "The legal footing is the Information Technology Act, 2000, which gives legal recognition to electronic records and to electronic and digital signatures, so an e-file is admissible in the same way as paper. The Public Records Act, 1993 governs the management and disposal of central government public records. In practice the working system is NIC's eOffice, whose eFile module carries the electronic file through the same noting and approval route as a physical file.\n\nFour qualities have to survive: authenticity (it is what it claims and came from who it claims), integrity (unaltered - protected by audit trails, version control and digital signatures), reliability, and usability (still retrievable and readable years later).\n\nMetadata is what makes this work. File number, subject, creator, date, department, security classification and retention category are what let a record be found, proved and disposed of correctly. Without them a scanned PDF is only a file on a disk.\n\nDigital preservation has problems paper does not: file format obsolescence, media decay, and dependence on software that no longer exists. The answers are migration to open standard formats such as PDF/A, periodic refresh of storage media, and checksums to detect silent corruption.",
    "facts": [
      "The IT Act, 2000 gives legal recognition to electronic records and to electronic and digital signatures",
      "The Public Records Act, 1993 governs the management, retention and disposal of central government public records",
      "Authenticity, integrity, reliability and usability are the four qualities an electronic record must retain",
      "Audit trails, version control and digital signatures are the standard controls for proving record integrity",
      "PDF/A is the preferred format for long-term preservation because it is self-contained and embeds its fonts",
      "NIC's eOffice, through its eFile module, is the standard electronic file system in Indian government offices"
    ],
    "traps": [
      "A record is not just any document - a record evidences a transaction and is fixed once created; a draft is still a working document",
      "Backup is not archiving - a backup is a short-term copy taken for recovery; archiving moves a record into preservation with metadata and a retention rule",
      "Digitisation is not records management - scanning paper without indexing, metadata and a retention category produces images, not managed records",
      "Deleting the visible copy is not disposal - the record survives in backups, mailboxes and shared drives until every copy is authorised and destroyed"
    ],
    "mnem": "",
    "rel": [
      "Digital India and e-Governance",
      "Data Privacy and Personal Data Protection",
      "Backup Concepts"
    ]
