    "id": "c165",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "IPv4 and IPv6",
    "def": "IPv4 is the 32-bit Internet Protocol addressing scheme written as four decimal octets separated by dots, such as 192.168.1.1, giving about 4.3 billion addresses. IPv6 is its 128-bit successor, written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits separated by colons. IPv6 exists because the IPv4 address space ran out.",
    "exp": "An IPv4 address is 32 bits: four octets, each 0 to 255. Two to the power 32 is roughly 4.3 billion addresses, and the global free pool was exhausted at the top level in 2011. NAT, private address ranges and CIDR stretched IPv4's life but did not solve the shortage.\n\nAn IPv6 address is 128 bits, written as eight hextets, for example 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329. Two shorthand rules apply: leading zeros inside a hextet may be dropped, and one run of consecutive all-zero hextets may be replaced by a double colon, but only once per address. That example compresses to 2001:db8::ff00:42:8329. The IPv6 loopback address is ::1, against IPv4's 127.0.0.1.\n\nOther examinable differences: IPv6 has no broadcast at all - it uses unicast, multicast and anycast, with multicast doing broadcast's work. IPv6 has a fixed-length header and no header checksum, so routers forward faster. An IPv6 host can configure its own address without a DHCP server (stateless autoconfiguration). IPv6 uses Neighbour Discovery in place of ARP.\n\nThe two are not directly interoperable, so migration uses dual-stack hosts, tunnelling IPv6 inside IPv4, and translation. India is among the world's highest-adoption IPv6 countries, driven mainly by mobile networks.",
    "facts": [
      "IPv4 is 32 bits (about 4.3 billion addresses); IPv6 is 128 bits.",
      "IPv4 uses dotted decimal notation; IPv6 uses colon-separated hexadecimal.",
      "IPv6 has no broadcast address; multicast and anycast replace it.",
      "The double colon may appear only once in an IPv6 address.",
      "IPv6 loopback is ::1; IPv4 loopback is 127.0.0.1.",
      "IPv6 supports stateless address autoconfiguration, so a host can address itself without DHCP."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "IPv6 is 128 bits, not 64 and not 128 bytes. Candidates routinely halve or double the figure.",
      "IPv6 is hexadecimal separated by colons, not decimal separated by dots.",
      "There is no deployed IPv5; the version number jumps from 4 to 6.",
      "Any option referring to an 'IPv6 broadcast address' is wrong by construction - IPv6 dropped broadcast."
    ],
    "mnem": "Four has dots and decimal; six has colons and hex.",
    "rel": [
      "IP Addressing",
      "NAT",
      "Subnetting"
    ]
