    "id": "c167",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Subnetting",
    "def": "Subnetting is dividing one IP network into several smaller logical networks by borrowing bits from the host portion of the address and adding them to the network portion. The subnet mask records where the network/host boundary now falls, and is written either in dotted decimal or as a CIDR prefix length.",
    "exp": "A subnet mask is 32 bits: contiguous 1s covering the network portion, contiguous 0s covering the host portion. A host ANDs a destination address with its own mask to get the network address, and that is exactly how it decides whether to deliver directly on the LAN or hand the packet to its default gateway.\n\nBorrowing n host bits creates 2 to the power n subnets. If h host bits remain, each subnet holds 2 to the power h addresses, of which 2^h minus 2 are assignable, because the all-zeros and all-ones host values are the network and broadcast addresses.\n\nWorked example. Take 192.168.1.0/24 and borrow 2 bits. You get /26, mask 255.255.255.192: four subnets of 64 addresses, 62 usable hosts each. The ranges are 192.168.1.0-63, .64-127, .128-191 and .192-255. In the first, 192.168.1.0 is the network address, 192.168.1.63 is the broadcast, and .1 to .62 are hosts. The block size 64 is 256 minus the mask's interesting octet (256 - 192), and subnets always start at multiples of the block size.\n\nWhy subnet: contain broadcast traffic, separate departments for security, and stop wasting addresses. VLSM extends this by letting different subnets of the same network carry different prefix lengths.",
    "facts": [
      "Borrowing n bits creates 2^n subnets; h remaining host bits give 2^h minus 2 usable hosts per subnet.",
      "The network address is the bitwise AND of the IP address and the subnet mask.",
      "/26 is mask 255.255.255.192: four subnets of 64 addresses with 62 usable hosts each.",
      "Block size equals 256 minus the value of the interesting octet of the mask.",
      "A /30 leaves only 2 usable addresses, which is why it is the standard mask for point-to-point router links.",
      "A valid subnet mask is always contiguous 1s followed by contiguous 0s - no gaps."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Total addresses vs usable hosts: subtract 2 when asked for hosts, do not subtract when asked for the size of the block.",
      "More subnet bits means more subnets but fewer hosts in each. The two trade off directly against each other.",
      "A longer prefix is a smaller network. /26 is smaller than /24 even though the number is larger.",
      "Subnetting only moves the network/host boundary. It does not change the address class or whether the address is public or private."
    ],
    "mnem": "Longer prefix, smaller network, fewer hosts.",
    "rel": [
      "IP Addressing",
      "Routing and Switching",
      "IPv4 and IPv6"
    ]
