    "id": "c170",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Switches",
    "def": "A switch is a layer-2 (Data Link) device that connects devices within a LAN and forwards Ethernet frames using MAC addresses. It learns which MAC address sits behind which port, stores that in a MAC address table (also called the CAM table), and delivers a known unicast frame only to the destination port instead of to every port.",
    "exp": "Three functions define a switch. Learning: record the source MAC of each incoming frame against the port it arrived on. Forwarding and filtering: send a known unicast out only its own port, and discard it if source and destination are on the same port. Flooding: send broadcasts, multicasts and unknown unicasts out of every other port. Redundant links are handled by Spanning Tree Protocol, which blocks alternate paths so frames do not loop forever.\n\nEach switch port is its own collision domain, so on full-duplex links collisions effectively disappear and every port gets dedicated bandwidth rather than a share. But by default all ports remain in one broadcast domain, so a broadcast still reaches everyone. VLANs are how you split that broadcast domain without adding a router.\n\nForwarding methods differ in how much of the frame is read before forwarding. Store-and-forward takes the whole frame and checks the CRC first: slowest but it never forwards a corrupt frame. Cut-through reads only the destination MAC and starts sending immediately: fastest, but it forwards errors too. Fragment-free reads the first 64 bytes as a compromise.\n\nAn unmanaged switch simply works. A managed switch adds VLANs, port mirroring, link aggregation and SNMP. A layer-3 switch adds IP routing between VLANs.",
    "facts": [
      "A switch operates at OSI layer 2 and forwards on MAC addresses held in its MAC or CAM table.",
      "Every switch port is a separate collision domain; all ports share one broadcast domain unless VLANs are configured.",
      "A switch's three functions are learning, forwarding/filtering and flooding; Spanning Tree Protocol prevents loops.",
      "Forwarding modes: store-and-forward (checks the CRC), cut-through (reads the destination MAC only), fragment-free (reads the first 64 bytes).",
      "A layer-3 switch can route between VLANs; a plain layer-2 switch cannot.",
      "Port mirroring on a managed switch is how traffic is copied to a monitoring station."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Switch vs hub: the switch forwards a known unicast to one port, the hub broadcasts to all. Only the hub is the answer to 'broadcasts to every port'.",
      "A switch breaks collision domains but not broadcast domains. Routers and VLANs break broadcast domains.",
      "A switch floods, rather than drops, a frame whose destination MAC it has not yet learned. Flooding is normal behaviour, not a fault.",
      "Switches use MAC addresses and routers use IP addresses. A layer-2 switch holds no routing table and no IP forwarding logic."
    ],
    "mnem": "A switch does three things: Learn, Forward, Flood.",
    "rel": [
      "Hubs",
      "Routing and Switching",
      "Network Monitoring"
    ]
