    "id": "c175",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "DNS",
    "def": "The Domain Name System (DNS) is the distributed directory service that resolves human-readable domain names such as mizoram.gov.in into IP addresses, and back. It is a hierarchy: a root level, top-level domains (.in, .com), then delegated zones. DNS normally runs over UDP port 53, falling back to TCP port 53 for large responses and zone transfers.",
    "exp": "Resolution works through two kinds of server. A recursive resolver is the one your PC is configured with; it does the chasing on your behalf, asking a root server, then the .in servers, then the servers for the zone, and returns a single final answer. An authoritative server is the one that actually holds the zone file for a domain and answers with authority for it. Your ISP or office resolver is recursive; the organisation's own name server is authoritative for its domain.\n\nEvery record carries a TTL, the number of seconds a resolver may cache it. Short TTLs make changes propagate quickly; long TTLs cut query load. A recently changed record may still resolve to the old address until the TTL expires everywhere — that is what people mean by propagation delay.\n\nThe record types to know: A maps a name to an IPv4 address, AAAA to an IPv6 address, CNAME makes one name an alias of another, MX names the mail server for a domain with a preference value, NS delegates a zone to its name servers, and PTR maps an address back to a name for reverse lookups.\n\nOperationally, DNS failure looks exactly like the network being down for users, even when routing is fine.",
    "facts": [
      "DNS uses port 53, over UDP for ordinary queries and TCP for zone transfers and oversized responses.",
      "A recursive resolver queries other servers on the client's behalf; an authoritative server holds the zone and answers for it directly.",
      "A record types: A (IPv4), AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias), MX (mail exchanger, with a preference value), NS (delegation), PTR (reverse lookup).",
      "TTL is the time in seconds a record may be cached by resolvers.",
      "The namespace is hierarchical: root, then top-level domains, then delegated zones.",
      "nslookup and dig are the standard tools for querying DNS directly."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Recursive vs authoritative — the resolver you configure on a client is recursive; it is not authoritative for anything. Only the domain's own name servers are authoritative.",
      "A CNAME is an alias to another name, not to an IP address. A record pointing straight to an IP is an A or AAAA record.",
      "MX gives the mail server's name, not its IP, and the lower preference number is preferred.",
      "Changes do not take effect instantly — cached copies live until their TTL expires. Flushing your own cache does not flush anyone else's."
    ],
    "mnem": "MX = Mail eXchanger; the smaller the preference number, the higher the priority.",
