    "id": "c188",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Remote Desktop Services",
    "def": "Remote Desktop Services is the Windows Server role that lets users run a full Windows desktop or a single published application on the server while interacting with it from their own device, with only screen updates, keystrokes and mouse movement crossing the network. It uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP), which listens on TCP port 3389. The two-session administrative form built into Windows Server is Remote Desktop for Administration; RDS proper is the licensed multi-user deployment.",
    "exp": "A full deployment separates distinct roles. The Session Host actually runs the users' sessions. The Connection Broker directs each user to the right host and reconnects a disconnected user to their existing session rather than starting a new one. RD Web Access publishes the available desktops and applications through a browser. The RD Gateway lets remote users tunnel RDP inside HTTPS, so port 3389 need never face the internet. RD Licensing issues the client access licences, which are sold per user or per device.\n\nThe attraction for a government office is central control. Applications and data stay on the server, so an old or low-specification PC still works as a client, backup covers one machine instead of forty, and a departing employee's access ends with their account. RemoteApp publishes a single application that appears in its own window on the user's desktop instead of an entire remote desktop.\n\nOn security: never expose RDP directly to the internet. Brute-forced RDP is one of the most common entry points for ransomware. Put it behind an RD Gateway or a VPN, and enable Network Level Authentication so the user must authenticate before any session is created on the server.",
    "facts": [
      "RDP uses TCP port 3389.",
      "RDS components include the Session Host, Connection Broker, RD Web Access, RD Gateway and RD Licensing.",
      "RDS client access licences are issued per user or per device.",
      "RemoteApp publishes an individual application in its own window rather than a full remote desktop.",
      "RD Gateway tunnels RDP inside HTTPS so that port 3389 need not be exposed externally.",
      "Network Level Authentication requires the user to authenticate before a session is created on the server."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Remote Desktop for Administration (a small fixed number of admin sessions, no RDS licence needed) is not the same as a full RDS deployment for many users, which requires RDS CALs.",
      "An RDS CAL does not replace the Windows Server CAL. A user needs both.",
      "RD Gateway versus VPN - the Gateway carries RDP inside HTTPS to a specific server; a VPN places the client on the network generally. They solve the same exposure problem differently.",
      "Remote Desktop is not Remote Assistance. Remote Assistance shares the user's own existing session so both parties see the same screen; RDP creates a separate session the user cannot watch."
    ],
    "mnem": "",
    "rel": [
      "VPN",
      "Windows Server Concepts",
      "Cloud Infrastructure"
    ]
