    "id": "c191",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "I",
    "unitTitle": "Computer Networking",
    "sub": "Microsoft Azure Fundamentals (Concept)",
    "def": "Microsoft Azure is Microsoft's public cloud platform, offering compute, storage, networking and identity services on a pay-per-use basis from data centres worldwide. The syllabus marks this topic \"(Concept)\", so what is examinable is how Azure is organised — regions, availability zones, subscriptions and resource groups — and the broad service categories, not individual product names.",
    "exp": "The physical layout has two levels. A region is a geographic area containing one or more data centres; you choose it when creating a resource, and it decides latency for your users and where the data resides. Within a region, availability zones are physically separate data centres with independent power, cooling and networking. Spreading an application across zones keeps it running when one data centre fails; recovering from the loss of a whole region needs a second region.\n\nThe management hierarchy runs downward. An account holds one or more subscriptions. A subscription is the billing and boundary unit — costs accumulate against it and service limits apply per subscription. Inside a subscription, resources sit in resource groups. A resource group is a logical container for the resources of one application or department: permissions are applied at group level, and deleting the group deletes everything in it. Every resource belongs to exactly one resource group.\n\nFour service categories cover the rest. Compute: virtual machines, container hosting, serverless functions. Storage: object, file and disk storage plus managed databases. Networking: virtual networks, load balancing, gateways and links back to your own premises. Identity: the cloud directory service that authenticates users and applications and governs access to everything else.\n\nAzure spans all three service models — virtual machines are IaaS, managed app and database services are PaaS, and Microsoft's cloud office suite is SaaS on the same identity platform. Azure operates data centre regions in India.",
    "facts": [
      "A region is a geographic area of one or more Azure data centres; availability zones are physically separate data centres within a region with independent power, cooling and networking.",
      "A subscription is the billing boundary; service limits and quotas apply per subscription.",
      "A resource group is a logical container for related resources — every resource belongs to exactly one resource group, and deleting the group deletes its contents.",
      "The main Azure service categories are compute, storage, networking and identity.",
      "Azure virtual machines are IaaS; managed application and database services are PaaS."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "Region and availability zone are different scales. Zones protect against a single data centre failure inside one region; surviving the loss of an entire region needs a second region.",
      "A resource group is a management and permissions container, not a network or security boundary — resources in different resource groups can still talk to each other over the network.",
      "Subscription and account are not the same. One account can hold several subscriptions, and the subscription is what carries the billing and the quotas.",
      "Choosing a region is not only about latency — it also determines where the data physically resides, which matters for government data-residency requirements."
    ],
    "mnem": "Downward: Account, Subscription, Resource group, Resource.",
    "rel": [
      "Cloud Infrastructure",
      "Virtual Networks",
      "Windows Server Concepts"
    ]
