    "id": "c217",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "II",
    "unitTitle": "Database Management System",
    "sub": "NoSQL Databases (Introduction)",
    "def": "NoSQL databases are non-relational data stores that drop the fixed table-and-row schema and, usually, part of the strict consistency guarantee of a relational system, in exchange for horizontal scale-out across many ordinary servers and a schema that can change without a migration. The name is read as 'Not Only SQL', not 'no SQL at all'.",
    "exp": "There are four families. Key-value stores fetch a value by its key alone, like a dictionary — session data, caching, shopping carts; Redis. Document stores hold self-describing JSON-style documents whose fields may vary from one document to the next — product catalogues, content management, user profiles; MongoDB. Column-family or wide-column stores group columns into families and absorb very heavy write volumes across a cluster — time-series, sensor and log data; Cassandra. Graph databases treat nodes and the relationships between them as first-class and traverse those relationships directly — social networks, fraud-ring detection, recommendation; Neo4j.\n\nThey exist because scaling a relational database mainly means scaling up onto a bigger machine, and because joins and a fixed schema are awkward when data volume is enormous and its shape keeps changing. The honest trade-off is stated as BASE — Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency — the counterpart to relational ACID: a read taken immediately after a write may return the old value, and the copies converge shortly afterwards.\n\nNoSQL is not better than SQL. It makes a different trade, and money, ledgers and anything needing guaranteed integrity across related tables still belong in a relational database. The line has also blurred: relational engines now store JSON, and several NoSQL products offer tunable or strong consistency.",
    "facts": [
      "The four families of NoSQL databases are key-value, document, column-family (wide-column) and graph.",
      "NoSQL stands for 'Not Only SQL'; many NoSQL products expose SQL-like query languages.",
      "BASE (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency) is the NoSQL counterpart to the relational ACID properties.",
      "NoSQL systems scale horizontally by adding commodity servers, whereas relational systems traditionally scale vertically with a bigger machine.",
      "Typical examples by family: Redis (key-value), MongoDB (document), Cassandra (column-family), Neo4j (graph).",
      "Graph databases store relationships as first-class objects, which makes traversing connections faster than repeated relational joins."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "NoSQL is not 'better than SQL'. It trades strict consistency and join power for scale and schema flexibility; transactional and financial data still belongs in a relational database.",
      "NoSQL does not mean 'no SQL language'. It means not only SQL — the defining break is with the relational model, not with the query language.",
      "Eventual consistency vs strict consistency — under eventual consistency a read straight after a write can return stale data. A relational system guarantees you read what you just wrote.",
      "ACID vs BASE — ACID is Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability (relational); BASE is Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency (NoSQL). Do not mix the expansions."
    ],
    "mnem": "The four families: Key, Document, Column, Graph. And chemistry gives the trade-off free — relational is ACID, NoSQL is BASE.",
    "rel": [
      "Relational Database Management System",
      "Transactions",
      "Concurrency Control"
    ]
