    "id": "c218",
    "paper": "TECH2",
    "unit": "II",
    "unitTitle": "Database Management System",
    "sub": "Normalisation",
    "def": "Normalisation is the stepwise process of restructuring a relation, using its functional dependencies, so that redundant data and the insertion, deletion and update anomalies that redundancy causes are removed. Each normal form adds one rule to the one before it, so a relation in 3NF is necessarily in 2NF and 1NF as well.",
    "exp": "A functional dependency X determines Y means that each value of X fixes exactly one value of Y. Normalisation works by finding dependencies that point somewhere other than at a whole candidate key, and splitting the table so they do.\n\nFirst Normal Form requires every attribute to be atomic: one value per cell, and no repeating groups such as Skill1, Skill2, Skill3. A column holding two phone numbers breaks it.\n\nSecond Normal Form adds: no non-key attribute may depend on only PART of a composite key. In ENROLMENT(StudentID, CourseID, StudentName), StudentName depends on StudentID alone, which is a partial dependency. Note that a relation whose key is a single attribute is automatically in 2NF, because there is no part of the key to depend on.\n\nThird Normal Form adds: no non-key attribute may depend on another non-key attribute. In EMPLOYEE(EmpID, EmpName, DeptID, DeptName), EmpID determines DeptID and DeptID determines DeptName, so DeptName reaches the key only indirectly. That is a transitive dependency.\n\nBoyce-Codd Normal Form tightens 3NF to: every determinant must be a candidate key. It only bites when a relation has overlapping composite candidate keys, which is why a relation can be in 3NF and still fail BCNF.\n\nWork upward and stop at the first rule broken; the highest normal form a relation satisfies is one step below that. Denormalisation, reintroducing redundancy deliberately for read performance, is a design choice and not a failure.",
    "facts": [
      "1NF: every attribute atomic, no repeating groups.",
      "2NF: 1NF plus no partial dependency of a non-key attribute on part of a composite key.",
      "3NF: 2NF plus no transitive dependency of one non-key attribute on another.",
      "BCNF: every determinant is a candidate key — stricter than 3NF.",
      "A relation whose primary key is a single attribute is automatically in 2NF.",
      "Normalisation removes insertion, deletion and update anomalies caused by redundancy."
    ],
    "traps": [
      "2NF violations require a COMPOSITE key. If the key is one attribute, partial dependency is impossible and the relation cannot fail 2NF.",
      "Partial dependency (part of the key) and transitive dependency (via another non-key attribute) are different faults, removed by different normal forms. Both are always offered.",
      "3NF does not imply BCNF. A relation with overlapping candidate keys can satisfy 3NF and still fail BCNF — the standard example is CLASS(StudentID, Subject, Teacher) with Teacher determining Subject.",
      "The highest normal form is one step BELOW the first rule broken, so work upward from 1NF rather than guessing from the shape of the table.",
      "Denormalisation is a deliberate performance trade-off, not a normalisation error."
    ],
    "mnem": "1NF atomic, 2NF whole key, 3NF nothing but the key, BCNF every determinant a key.",
    "rel": [
      "Relational Database Management System",
      "ER Modelling",
      "Data Models"
    ],
    "derived": true,
    "prov": "The official syllabus does not list this as a subtopic. It is added because past questions for this post reach it and the syllabus states that questions may come from other topics prescribed for the post's educational qualification. Derived, not official."
